`apiHandler` can return `{ success: true }` even when no dataSource method actually ran (e.g., `dataSource.execute` is missing and `params.recordId` isn’t provided / `dataSource.update` isn’t available). This makes actions appear to succeed (including success toast) while doing nothing. Also, when falling back to `dataSource.update`, the update payload currently includes `recordId`, which is being used as the id argument and generally shouldn’t be persisted as a field value—strip it from the update data.
```suggestion
            let executed = false;

            // Generic list-level API handler: update/execute via dataSource
            if (typeof dataSource.execute === 'function') {
                await dataSource.execute(objectDef.name, target, params);
                executed = true;
            } else if (params.recordId && Object.keys(params).length > 1 && typeof dataSource.update === 'function') {
                const { recordId, ...updateData } = params as { recordId: string } & Record<string, unknown>;
                await dataSource.update(objectDef.name, recordId, updateData);
                executed = true;
            }

            if (!executed) {
                return { success: false, error: 'No dataSource handler available for action' };
            }
```

`ComponentRegistry.register(...)` without a namespace triggers `console.warn` (by design in `packages/core/src/registry/Registry.ts`), which can clutter test output. Consider registering these test-only components with a namespace meta (e.g., `{ namespace: 'test' }`) and/or silencing `console.warn` in this suite.
```suggestion
    ComponentRegistry.register(
      'object-grid',
      (props: any) => <div data-testid="object-grid">Grid View: {props.schema.objectName}</div>,
      { namespace: 'test' }
    );
    ComponentRegistry.register(
      'list-view',
      (_props: any) => <div data-testid="list-view">List View</div>,
      { namespace: 'test' }
    );
```
